Status {#status}
================

This is an **exploratory draft** of a runtime-neutral diagnostics channel API for server-side JavaScript, intended for consideration by Ecma TC55 (WinterTC). It specifies the <dfn>channel primitive</dfn>: named publish-subscribe for structured diagnostic events. It deliberately does not yet specify the tracing lifecycle layer ([[#tracingchannel-layer]]).

This draft accompanies a [divergence matrix](https://github.com/bengl/proposal-diagnostics-channels/tree/main/divergence-matrix), a probe suite run against Node, Deno, Bun, and Cloudflare Workers. The matrix is the evidence base: where this document and the matrix disagree, the matrix wins, and this document is wrong.

Scope {#scope}
=============

Server-side JavaScript runtimes need a common, low-overhead way for libraries and runtimes to emit structured diagnostic lifecycle events without monkey-patching, import hooks, or vendor-specific APIs. Node defined such an API, `node:diagnostics_channel`, and the other major runtimes implement it for compatibility. The portability is accidental: nothing specifies channel semantics, subscriber ordering, what untrusted code can subscribe to, or which channel names are safe to depend on. This specification specifies the semantics, with `node:diagnostics_channel` as the primary existing instance.

Instrumentation vendors (OpenTelemetry, Sentry, Datadog) consume these channels today with per-runtime glue. A standard surface makes one integration sufficient.

Conformance {#conformance}
==========================

A conforming implementation provides a <dfn>diagnostics channel module</dfn>, a module whose exported members are as defined in [[#module]] and whose behavior is as specified in [[#operations]]. The module's specifier is implementation-defined. This specification does not standardize the `node:` prefix. A runtime's `node:diagnostics_channel` is a conforming instance to the extent its behavior matches this specification.

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [[!RFC2119]] [[!RFC8179]] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.

A conforming implementation MAY provide extensions, such as additional exports, that do not contradict anything this specification defines.

The diagnostics channel module {#module}
=======================================

The module exports the following members:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Member</th><th>Signature</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>channel(name)</code></td><td><code>Channel channel((DOMString or symbol) name)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>subscribe(name, onMessage)</code></td><td><code>undefined subscribe((DOMString or symbol) name, Function onMessage)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>unsubscribe(name, onMessage)</code></td><td><code>boolean unsubscribe((DOMString or symbol) name, Function onMessage)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>hasSubscribers(name)</code></td><td><code>boolean hasSubscribers((DOMString or symbol) name)</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="note">
The signatures use Web IDL notation for precision, but they are not Web IDL definitions. This specification does not define IDL interfaces for the module surface, and it does not require the module to be exposed as a Web IDL namespace or global.
</div>

The Channel object {#channel-object}
------------------------------------

A <dfn>Channel</dfn> is an object, supplied by the implementation, with the following members:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Member</th><th>Kind</th><th>Value</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>read-only data property</td><td>The channel's name, a String or Symbol</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>hasSubscribers</code></td><td>read-only data property</td><td><code>true</code> if the [=subscriber list=] is non-empty, otherwise <code>false</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>subscribe(onMessage)</code></td><td>method</td><td>The same operation as the module-level <code>subscribe()</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>unsubscribe(onMessage)</code></td><td>method</td><td>The same operation as the module-level <code>unsubscribe()</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>publish(message)</code></td><td>method</td><td>Publishes <var>message</var> to the channel ([[#op-publish]])</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Each distinct name has at most one Channel. The <code>channel(name)</code> function returns the same object on every call with the same name.

Operations {#operations}
========================

To <dfn>resolve a channel</dfn> given |name|: return the Channel for |name|, creating one if none exists.

A <dfn>subscriber list</dfn> is an ordered list of functions. New subscribers are appended. The order of the list is its <dfn>subscription order</dfn>.

The channel(name) function {#op-channel}
---------------------------------------

1. If |name| is neither a String nor a Symbol, throw a `TypeError`.
2. [=Resolve a channel=] given |name|, and return it.

The subscribe(name, onMessage) function {#op-subscribe}
------------------------------------------------------

1. If |name| is neither a String nor a Symbol, throw a `TypeError`.
2. If |onMessage| is not callable, throw a `TypeError`.
3. [=Resolve a channel=] given |name|.
4. If |onMessage| is already in the [=subscriber list=], do nothing and return `undefined`.
5. Append |onMessage| to the end of the [=subscriber list=], and return `undefined`.

The unsubscribe(name, onMessage) function {#op-unsubscribe}
-----------------------------------------------------------

1. [=Resolve a channel=] given |name|.
2. If |onMessage| is in the [=subscriber list=], remove it and return `true`.
3. Return `false`.

The hasSubscribers(name) function {#op-hassubscribers}
-------------------------------------------------------

1. [=Resolve a channel=] given |name|.
2. Return `true` if the [=subscriber list=] is non-empty, otherwise `false`.

The `hasSubscribers()` function exists so that emitters can avoid constructing expensive diagnostic payloads when nobody is listening. A channel that has no subscribers MUST NOT cause user code to run when published to.

The publish(message) method {#op-publish}
-----------------------------------------

1. Let |channel| be the [=Channel=] this method was called on.
2. If |channel|'s [=subscriber list=] is empty, return `undefined` without further action. The message is not inspected and no user code runs.
3. For each |subscriber| in |channel|'s [=subscriber list=], in [=subscription order=]: invoke |subscriber| with |message| as the first argument and |channel|'s name as the second argument. Each invocation completes, including all of its synchronous side effects, before the next begins.
4. Return `undefined`.

Publishing is synchronous: when `publish()` returns, every subscriber that is invoked by it has completed. This document does not define what happens if a subscriber throws ([[#open-questions]]).

Subscriber errors {#subscriber-errors}
======================================

This draft deliberately does not specify what happens when a subscriber throws. The probe suite recorded three distinct behaviors: Node and Bun defer the error to the uncaught exception path and continue dispatching to later subscribers, Deno 2.9.7 delivers the error only to an `uncaughtException` listener registered before the publish and otherwise drops it, and Workers propagates the throw synchronously out of `publish()` and skips the remaining subscribers. A future revision of this document will pick one. Until then, portable instrumentation code must not rely on any of the three behaviors.

The TracingChannel layer {#tracingchannel-layer}
================================================

<div class="note">
This section is informative and does not define any conformance requirements.
</div>

Node's `TracingChannel` models one logical operation as five events, `start`, `end`, `asyncStart`, `asyncEnd`, and `error`, with one shared context object threaded through them, and store binding that restores an async context across the async boundary. The probe suite found the five-event lifecycle, event order, context identity, and store restoration identical across Node, Deno, Bun, and Workers.

This layer is not specified here for one reason: its store binding is shaped by async context propagation, and TC39's `AsyncContext` proposal (Stage 2) is the standard home for that. The tracing layer should be specified against `AsyncContext` once its timeline is settled, rather than frozen against Node's current `AsyncLocalStorage` semantics.

Well-known channel names {#well-known-names}
============================================

<div class="note">
This section is informative and does not define any conformance requirements.
</div>

A future revision may define a registry of well-known channel names, starting with `http.server.request` and related exchange events, aligned with OpenTelemetry semantic conventions so vendor mapping is trivial. The [Fetch Server proposal](https://github.com/jasnell/proposal-fetch-server) would be the first consumer: server-exchange channels are the first well-known names with a live customer, per [its issue #7](https://github.com/jasnell/proposal-fetch-server/issues/7).

Security considerations {#security}
====================================

Channels cross trust boundaries. A subscriber observes the full content of every message published to its channel, including potentially sensitive payloads, and runs in-process with the publisher's privileges. What untrusted code may subscribe to, and any cross-isolate forwarding, are an open question ([[#open-questions]]). One runtime, Cloudflare Workers, forwards built-in channels to Tail Workers through structured clone, which is prior art for the isolation boundary such a rule needs.

The `hasSubscribers()` function reduces incidental exposure: an emitter that builds sensitive payloads can skip building them when nobody is listening.

Open questions {#open-questions}
================================

* **Subscriber errors.** Three behaviors are observed in the wild ([[#subscriber-errors]]). This draft leaves the requirement open.
* **Duplicate subscription.** This draft says a second `subscribe()` with the same function has no effect. That position is unverified against the runtimes and should be probed before this document advances.
* **Publish during dispatch.** Whether `publish()` may be called from within a subscriber of the same channel is unspecified.
* **Realm and agent scoping.** Whether channels are shared across realms or agents of one runtime, and what that means for isolation, is unspecified.
* **Module specifier.** Whether TC55 standardizes a specifier for the [=diagnostics channel module=], leaving `node:diagnostics_channel` as one conforming instance, or leaves the specifier entirely to runtimes.
* **Bounded channels.** Node 26 exports `BoundedChannel` and `boundedChannel`, absent in Node 22 and 24. The export surface is still moving even within Node. Whatever this specification pins has to allow the surface to grow.
